{"author":{"id":"038d65070aa747b6bc9d50939fa697ec871a40ed259db54429b255bbef2581d6"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792500150,"nonce":"eYBnjd4TueK8jIeFB9orGCcjB1Y=","title":"Reduce native layout cost when long Markdown blocks first mount","message":"Follow-up to 36d62b1218e9ac8262f16f1eaa60ca23b459e40101366f463871cabc52b1640f. Directional buffering, one-block construction, within-layout anchoring and measurement-only source text remove visible gaps and substantially reduce first-mount stalls, but long paragraphs still exceed a 60 Hz UI budget on their first native layout.\n\nReproducer: scripts/markdown-scroll-bench.sh --native (also LIGHT=1). The standalone production MarkdownView uses 250 varying paragraphs and phased down/up/tail/cold-up gestures. AOT measurements: down p95 21.84 ms / worst 29.41 ms; up 20.58 / 28.12; cold-up 19.93 / 27.91. The slow tail is p95 0.78 / worst 0.84 ms. All four phases have zero missing visible blocks and zero anchor jumps, with at most eight mounted blocks. Stage records place the remaining mount spikes in native layout, not extension transport or RayScript worker execution. Raw records: bin/markdown-scroll-native.log, with a final snapshot under bin/markdown-scroll-native-snapshots.\n\nInvestigate Ashui's initial intrinsic text/layout work and InlineFlow initialization through the existing APIs. The source text is already unwrapped, absolute and zero-size; InlineFlow paints its own wrapped pieces. Keep styled runs, wrapping, links, math, image cleanup and inactive-tab resource disposal intact. Do not solve this by retaining every native block.","files":null}]}